# +Chunks, +Words, +Budget, -Term, -String
# +String, +Words, +Budget, -Counts
# +String, +Words, -Fewest
# ?Point, ?Length, ?Seed, ?Slack, ?Kind, ?Flip
#   * <module> A string cut into the fewest dictionary words
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 139 and 140, "Word Break", asked for the fewest
# words): a bit string and a dictionary of bit words; into how few words of
# the dictionary can the string be cut? Asked as the set of the numbers at
# most T of the words of the cuttings; T is the fewest, so the set is that
# one number.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style, with the prelude's
# bits_prefix, filter, length, concat and natural_difference (the API of
# round-37-directions.md), bits_prefix wrapped by the program with its
# arguments swapped and passed to filter partially applied:
#
#     starts = \s. \w. bits_prefix w s
#     choose = Y (\self. \ws. \f. ws {} (\w. \rest. { f w, self rest f }))
#     drop   = Y (\self. \n. \s. n s (\m. s nil (\_. \t. self m t)))
#     cut    = Y (\self. \s. \r. \k.
#                s (k r)
#                  (\_. \_. r {} (\p. choose (filter (starts s) Words)
#                                          (\w. self (drop (length w) s) p k))))
#     program = cut (concat Chunks) T (\r. difference T r)
#
# Words is the dictionary, a Scott list of Scott lists of Booleans; the
# string is given as concat of a list of chunks, cut at random places that
# have nothing to do with the words.
# Directions: D (bits_prefix reached through a wrapper of the program,
# partially applied and passed to filter; concat); E (the words compared
# with the string bit by bit); B (merging by value: the cuttings are
# exponentially many, the states are the rest of the string and the budget);
# G (the budget arithmetic, length); and the regression direction
# 4 of that list (the string, a list, is built by concat of chunks
# and not written out as conses, and the rests are spelled
# drop (length w) (drop ... (concat Chunks))).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the numbers c =< T such that the string
# is a concatenation of c words of the dictionary: segment_counts/4, the
# recursion of that definition over the positions of the string. The
# fewest, fewest_segments/3, is its least element when there is one; for T
# the fewest S(P) is {T}, and for T one less it is empty.
#
# THE LEMMAS, on the definitions of the library functions (contest_attractors
# attractor_source/2 and the prelude), not on their native references.
# ⌜n⌝ is the Scott unary numeral, ⌜0⌝ = λz.λs. z and ⌜n + 1⌝ = λz.λs. s ⌜n⌝,
# which unary/2 builds exactly; true = λt.λf. t and false = λt.λf. f; [x1,
# ..., xn] is the Scott list cons x1 (... (cons xn nil)), cons h t one β
# step from λn.λc. c h t; a bit string or word is a list of Booleans, true
# for 1 and false for 0. Every word of the dictionary is nonempty.
# - (Y) fixpoint G =β G (fixpoint G), two β steps.
# - (Prefix) bits_prefix = Y (λs.λa.λb. a true (λh.λt. b false
#   (λo.λr. h (o (s t r) false) (o false (s t r))))). By induction on the
#   list a, bits_prefix a b =β true when a is a prefix of b and false
#   otherwise: nil gives true; cons h t against nil gives false; against
#   cons o r, h (o X false) (o false X), X = bits_prefix t r, gives X when
#   h and o are the same Boolean and false otherwise (the four cases).
# - (Filter) filter = Y (λs.λp.λxs. xs nil (λh.λt. p h (cons h (s p t))
#   (s p t))). For a list xs and a p with p x =β true or false for every x
#   of xs, filter p xs =β the list of the x of xs, in order, with
#   p x =β true, by induction on xs. With p = starts s, s =β the string
#   u, p w →β bits_prefix w s, so filter (starts s) Words is β-equal to the
#   list of the words that are prefixes of u (Prefix).
# - (Length) length = Y (λs.λxs. xs ⌜0⌝ (λ_.λt. successor (s t))),
#   successor = λn.λz.λs. s n: length xs =β ⌜|xs|⌝, by induction on xs.
# - (Drop) drop ⌜n⌝ u =β the list u without its first n elements, for
#   n =< |u|, by induction on n: ⌜0⌝ gives u, ⌜m + 1⌝ with u = cons h t
#   gives drop ⌜m⌝ t.
# - (Concat) concat = foldr append nil, foldr f z xs = xs z (λh.λt. f h
#   (foldr f z t)), append = Y (λs.λxs.λys. xs ys (λh.λt. cons h (s t ys))).
#   append a b =β the list a followed by b, by induction on a; so
#   concat [c1, ..., cj] =β the list c1 followed by ... cj, by induction on
#   j.
# - (Choose) choose [w1, ..., wm] f =β {f w1, choose [w2, ..., wm] f}, and
#   choose nil f =β {}.
# - (Budget) r {} (λp. M) with r =β ⌜j⌝ is β-equal to {} when j = 0 and to
#   M[p := ⌜j - 1⌝] when j > 0.
# - (Difference) difference = Y (λs.λm.λn. n m (λn1. m ⌜0⌝ (λm1. s m1 n1))).
#   By induction on n, difference ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β ⌜m - n⌝ when m >= n.
# - (Empty) If U →* {} by β and η steps, no pure term is reached from U by
#   →c steps. Every →c step keeps U →* {}: a β or η step by confluence, {}
#   being normal; a member replacement of a set s by Lemma 2a of
#   certificate_meta_lemmas.md, which replays U →* {} to {} with the
#   descendants of s replaced, and {} is no descendant of s, s having a
#   member and descendants keeping the number of members. A pure term
#   reduces only to pure terms, and {} is not pure.
#
# So cut s r k, with s =β a suffix v of the string and r =β ⌜j⌝, is
# β-equal to k r when v is empty; when v is not, to {} when j = 0, and
# otherwise to {F w1, {F w2, ... {F wm, {}}}} for the words w1, ..., wm of
# the dictionary that are prefixes of v, in order, where F w is
# cut (drop (length w) s) ⌜j - 1⌝ k and drop (length w) s is β-equal to v
# without its first |w| elements (Y, Budget, Filter, Choose, Length, Drop).
#
# PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For a cutting of the string into c =< T words: the program is
#   β-equal to cut u ⌜T⌝ k, u the string (Concat). At the i-th word w,
#   the state is β-equal to cut v ⌜T - i + 1⌝ k, v the rest, and
#   T - i + 1 > 0; take the member F w of the nested sets above, by Lemma
#   2b.1 on the β-equal term: the state reached is β-equal to the next one.
#   At the end the rest is empty and the term is β-equal to k ⌜T - c⌝ =
#   difference ⌜T⌝ ⌜T - c⌝ =β ⌜c⌝ (Difference), pure and normal: ⌜c⌝ is in
#   that term's meaning (equal terms, Lemma 2b.2) and so in ⟦P⟧ (Lemma
#   2b.2).
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. Every set of P and of its reducts is one written in choose,
#   or {} of the budget or of the end of choose; each chosen branch is the
#   whole term, every call of cut being in tail position. Where the budget
#   is ⌜0⌝ before the end of the string, and at the end of choose's list,
#   the term is β-equal to {}, and no pure term is reached from it (Empty).
#   So a pure term is reached only along a sequence of words, each a prefix
#   of the rest of the string (Filter), each paid by the budget's successor
#   case (Budget), until the rest is empty: a cutting of the string into c
#   words, c =< T, and the term is then β-equal to difference ⌜T⌝ ⌜T - c⌝,
#   so to ⌜c⌝ (Difference).
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: segments_point/6 below. The traps: T one less
# than the fewest (empty), and a near miss, the string of a subtask with one
# bit flipped so that no cutting exists (empty for every T; a setting that
# answers some number there is wrong).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: the dynamic programme over the positions of the string,
# the fewest words to the end from each, the words found by string matching
# (D. E. Knuth, J. H. Morris, V. R. Pratt, Fast Pattern Matching in Strings,
# SIAM J. Comput. 1977, doi:10.1137/0206024); tabled evaluation keyed by the
# value of the rest and the budget (W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996,
# doi:10.1145/227595.227597).

#   THE SUBTASKS: segments_point(?Point, ?Length, ?Seed, ?Slack, ?Kind, ?Flip):
#   the dictionary and a string of about Length bits made of its words from
